  description: '''
    Collaborative analytics investigation that routes to the right Gartner analytics level based
    on the user's intent, then offers a contextual menu so the user steers the investigation.
    Replaces change_analysis v1.

    Auto-trigger on:
      • Observation — "What happened to revenue recently?", "Show me the trend in signups",
        "What's going on with MRR?", "How is revenue doing?"
      • Explanation — "Why did revenue drop?", "What's causing the decline in conversions?",
        "Explain the increase in churn last quarter"
      • Attribution — "Which region drove the drop?", "Break revenue down by product",
        "What segment is behind this?", "Where did the increase come from?"
      • Projection — "What will revenue look like next quarter?", "Forecast signups" (stub)
      • Recommendation — "What should we do about the churn increase?" (stub)

    Do NOT trigger for:
      • Pure anomaly scanning with no specific change in mind ("detect anomalies in revenue") — detect_anomaly handles those
      • Chart explanation ("what does this chart show?") — explain_and_insight handles those
  '''

  content: @md

    # Change Analysis v2

    This skill is a **collaborative partner** — it detects the user's intent, runs the right
    analysis, then offers a contextual escalation menu. The user picks what to explore next;
    the skill never auto-proceeds.

    ---

    ## On every invocation — check for a stage block first

    Scan the conversation history for the most recent **Change Analysis v2 stage block** (format
    defined in Stage Block Format below).

    **If found:** load state from it. Show the dynamic escalation menu for the most recently
    completed level (see Escalation Menus), then stop. Do not re-run any analysis.

    **If not found:** fresh invocation. Continue to Intent Detection.

    ---

    ## Intent detection — fresh invocations only

    Classify the user's question:

    | Signal | Intent | Entry point |
    |---|---|---|
    | "What happened?", "What's the trend?", "How is X doing?" | Observation | Level 1 — Descriptive |
    | "Why did X change?", "What's causing X?", "Explain X change" | Explanation | Level 2a — Anomaly check |
    | "Which segment drove X?", "Break X down by Y" | Attribution | Level 2c — Key driver (skip anomaly) |
    | "What will X look like?", "Forecast X" | Projection | Level 3 — Predictive stub |
    | "What should we do about X?" | Recommendation | Level 4 — Prescriptive stub |

    Ambiguous (e.g. "Tell me about revenue") → default to Level 1 Descriptive.

    **Required:** metric name. If missing, ask before doing anything:
    > "Which metric are you looking at?"

    ---

    ## Level 1 — Descriptive ("What happened?")

    Goal: show what the metric has been doing recently. No judgment yet.

    **Steps:**

    1. Resolve the metric and dataset (`list_datasets` / `fetch_dataset`).

    2. Generate a **CombinationChart** showing the actual metric as a solid line and a
       3-period moving average as a dashed trend line. This is the programmatic trend line —
       Holistics does not support adding trend lines declaratively to existing charts in AML.
       Use the same `calculation` block pattern as `detect_anomaly`:
       - `rolling_avg`: `window_avg(M, -3..-1, order: T | asc(), partition: [])`
       - Two series on the y-axis: `actual` (solid, primary color) and `rolling_avg` (dashed, grey)
       - If chart AML syntax is unclear, call `search_docs("CombinationChart calculation series AML")` first.
       - Run with `execute_viz` (pass only `dataset_uname`, `viz`, `title`; never an `aql` property).

       If the dataset has no suitable time dimension or the chart fails after one retry,
       fall back to `execute_aql` showing the metric over recent periods as a plain table.

    3. Output a 2–3 sentence plain-text summary:
       > "[Metric] has [been declining / rising / been flat] over the past [N] [periods].
       > It [peaked / bottomed] at [value] in [period] and [has dropped / risen] to [value]
       > in [latest period] — a [N]% [decline / increase]. The trend line shows
       > [a consistent downward slope / an upward acceleration / a leveling off]."

    4. Write a stage block (see Stage Block Format) with `Timeframe` set and
       `Completed diagnostics` empty. Show escalation **Menu A**, then stop.

    ---

    ## Level 2 — Diagnostic ("Why did it happen?")

    ### 2a. Anomaly check

    Call `load_skill("detect_anomaly")`. Let it run fully through all five steps.

    When detect_anomaly finishes, write a new stage block appending `anomaly_check` to
    `Completed diagnostics` and recording the `Anomaly verdict`.
    Show **Menu B** (anomalous) or **Menu C** (within normal range), then stop.

    ### 2b. YoY comparison

    **Period resolution:** use `Base period` from the stage block if already set; otherwise
    default to the most recently completed period at month granularity. Ask if granularity
    is ambiguous.

    Derive the comparison period: same period one year prior at the same granularity
    (e.g. `@(2026-06)` → `@(2025-06)`, `@(2026-Q2)` → `@(2025-Q2)`).

    Write an AQL query with both periods as separate measures, then render with `generate_viz`:

    - If the base period spans **multiple sub-periods** (e.g. Jan–Jun 2026 vs Jan–Jun 2025):
      use a **LineChart** with time on x-axis and two series (current year / prior year).
    - If comparing **two single values** (e.g. June 2026 vs June 2025):
      use a **ColumnChart** with period label on x-axis and the two values as two columns.

    Validate AQL with `validate_aql` before executing.

    Output a one-sentence verdict after the chart:
    > "[Metric] in [base period] is [value] — [N]% [above / below] the same period last year ([comparison value])."

    Write a new stage block appending `yoy_comparison`. Show the relevant menu, then stop.

    ### 2c. Dimensional breakdown

    Call `load_skill("key_driver")`. It reads `Base period` and `Comparison period` from
    the stage block.

    If those fields are absent (user jumped here from Descriptive without running anomaly
    check first): resolve periods using the same logic as 2b, write a new stage block with
    those fields set, then call `load_skill("key_driver")`.

    When key_driver finishes: write a new stage block appending `key_driver`.
    Show **Menu E**, then stop.

    ### 2d. Metric decomposition

    **Period resolution:** same as 2b/2c above.

    1. Inspect the metric formula using `fetch_dataset`.

    2. Classify the metric:
       - **Additive** (e.g. MRR = New MRR + Expansion − Churn) → each named component is a series
       - **Ratio** (e.g. Close Rate = Won / Total) → numerator and denominator are the two series
       - **Atomic** (no formula, single field) → inform the user:
         > "This metric has no components to decompose — try a dimensional breakdown instead."
         Show **Menu E** without appending to `Completed diagnostics`, then stop.

    3. Write one AQL query per component for both periods. Then render a single `execute_viz`:
       - **Additive** → `ColumnChart` with period on x-axis, metric value on y-axis,
         and component name as `Legend` (Holistics stacks/groups by Legend automatically).
       - **Ratio** → `ColumnChart` with period on x-axis and numerator / denominator as
         two series (use Legend or two separate y-axis measures).

       Validate AQL with `validate_aql` before executing.

    4. Output a plain-text summary identifying the component with the largest shift:
       - Additive example:
         > "MRR dropped $120K in June. New MRR was flat (+$2K), Expansion MRR declined $45K,
         > and Churn MRR increased $77K. Churn is the primary driver."
       - Ratio example:
         > "Close rate dropped from 32% to 24%. Won deals fell 18 (−22%), while total deals
         > held roughly flat (−3%). The drop is driven by fewer wins, not more losses entering
         > the funnel."

    Write a new stage block appending `decomposition`. Show **Menu F**, then stop.

    ---

    ## Level 3 — Predictive ("What will happen?") — stub

    > "Forecasting isn't supported yet — it's on the roadmap! In the meantime, would you like
    > to dig into what's been driving the trend?"

    Offer Level 2c (key driver) as an alternative. Do not write a stage block.

    ---

    ## Level 4 — Prescriptive ("What should we do?") — stub

    > "Recommended actions aren't supported yet — it's on the roadmap! Would you like to
    > investigate what's been driving the change first?"

    Offer Level 2a or 2c as alternatives. Do not write a stage block.

    ---

    ## Stage block format

    Write this as plain text after every level completes. Always write the **full current state**
    — do not reference or rely on a previous block. The most recent block in the conversation
    supersedes all earlier ones.

    ```
    ---
    Change Analysis v2 — stage
    - Metric: [metric name]
    - Dataset: [dataset_uname]
    - Timeframe: [e.g. "Jan 2025 – Jun 2026"]
    - Base period: [resolved literal, e.g. "@(2026-06)"]       ← omit if not yet resolved
    - Comparison period: [resolved literal, e.g. "@(2025-06)"] ← omit if not yet resolved
    - Anomaly verdict: [Anomalous / Within normal range]        ← omit if anomaly check not run
    - Completed diagnostics: [comma-separated: anomaly_check, yoy_comparison, key_driver, decomposition]
    ---
    ```

    ---

    ## Escalation menus

    Menus are **dynamic**: before rendering, read `Completed diagnostics` from the latest
    stage block and drop any option whose key already appears in that list.
    Re-number the remaining options sequentially.

    - If only one option remains → present it as a direct offer, not a numbered list.
    - If all four diagnostic keys are present → output the closing summary instead of a menu.

    The full option pools are listed below. Options in *(italics)* show the key used for
    completed-diagnostics tracking.

    **Menu A — after Descriptive:**
    > **Want to dig deeper?**
    > 1. Is this change normal? *(anomaly_check)*
    > 2. How does this compare to the same period last year? *(yoy_comparison)*
    > 3. Which segments are driving this? *(key_driver)*
    > 4. What will this look like going forward? *(Predictive — coming soon)*

    **Menu B — after anomaly check, anomalous:**
    > **The change is unusual. Want to find what drove it?**
    > 1. Break it down by dimensions *(key_driver)*
    > 2. Decompose the metric — see which component shifted *(decomposition)*
    > 3. Compare to the same period last year *(yoy_comparison)*
    > 4. What should we do? *(Prescriptive — coming soon)*

    **Menu C — after anomaly check, within normal range:**
    > **This looks like normal variation. Anything else you'd like to check?**
    > 1. Break it down by dimensions anyway *(key_driver)*
    > 2. Decompose the metric *(decomposition)*
    > 3. Compare to the same period last year *(yoy_comparison)*

    **Menu D — after YoY comparison:**
    > 1. Break it down by dimensions *(key_driver)*
    > 2. Decompose the metric *(decomposition)*
    > 3. Check if this is statistically unusual *(anomaly_check)*
    > 4. What should we do? *(Prescriptive — coming soon)*

    **Menu E — after dimensional breakdown:**
    > 1. Compare to the same period last year *(yoy_comparison)*
    > 2. Decompose the metric *(decomposition)*
    > 3. Check if this is statistically unusual *(anomaly_check)*
    > 4. What should we do? *(Prescriptive — coming soon)*

    **Menu F — after metric decomposition:**
    > 1. Break it down by dimensions to find which segment drove the component shift *(key_driver)*
    > 2. Compare to the same period last year *(yoy_comparison)*
    > 3. Check if this is statistically unusual *(anomaly_check)*
    > 4. What should we do? *(Prescriptive — coming soon)*

    **Closing summary — when all four diagnostic keys are in Completed diagnostics:**
    > Here's what we covered for **[metric]** [timeframe]:
    > - [one-line finding per completed diagnostic, in the order they were run]
    >
    > Let me know if you'd like to revisit anything.

    ---

    ## Guard rails

    - Never re-run a diagnostic already in `Completed diagnostics`
    - Always confirm the metric before any analysis if ambiguous
    - `detect_anomaly` and `key_driver` are called with `load_skill()`, never re-implemented inline
    - For YoY and decomposition: validate AQL with `validate_aql` before `execute_aql` / `execute_viz`
    - Pass only `dataset_uname`, `viz`, and `title` to `execute_viz` — never an `aql` property
    - The escalation menu is the only path to the next diagnostic — never auto-proceed

  ;;

}
